1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:10,850
A man on trial in Ohio told the court he reserved his rights "per UCC
1-308 without prejudice", and then added, "I give the Court no consent".

2
00:00:11,367 --> 00:00:17,317
He believed those words, said in the right place,
would leave the judge with no power over him.

3
00:00:17,333 --> 00:00:22,896
Is there anything you can say, sign or point to
that takes you out of a court's jurisdiction?

4
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:35,688
The people who sell this idea offer a whole kit: a code to write under your signature, a fringe
on the courtroom flag, an old act of Congress, and the theory that every law is just a contract.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,288
Each one leans on something real, so we can look at what it
rests on, and then read what courts did when someone tried it.

6
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:54,050
The kit starts with consent. A channel called Out of Babylon puts it plainly: "there are various
jurisdictions that do not have jurisdiction over you unless you grant them that jurisdiction".

7
00:00:54,067 --> 00:01:03,704
And the way to withhold that consent, according to The Grown Academy,
is to "write UCC 1-308 next to your signature All Rights Reserved".

8
00:01:03,733 --> 00:01:06,221
So what does that code
section actually say?

9
00:01:06,533 --> 00:01:22,446
It is part of the Uniform Commercial Code, and it says a party that "performs, promises performance, or assents to performance"
while explicitly reserving its rights does not lose them; words like "without prejudice" or "under protest" are enough.

10
00:01:22,467 --> 00:01:31,779
The official comment gives its purpose: the continuation of performance
"along the lines contemplated by the contract despite a pending dispute".

11
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:40,438
Picture a shop that keeps accepting a supplier's deliveries while it argues about
the bill, and doesn't want accepting them to count as dropping the argument.

12
00:01:40,767 --> 00:01:49,967
That is the whole job of the section: it is about performing a commercial
contract, and it says nothing about courts, police or criminal charges.

13
00:01:49,967 --> 00:01:59,617
And the man in Ohio? He was found guilty of all four charges, and the
appeals court noted that "Any arguments related to the UCC are unavailing".

14
00:02:00,633 --> 00:02:04,483
Withholding consent out loud fares
no better than writing it down.

15
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:15,250
In United States v. Benabe, a federal appeals court wrote that whatever status
a person claims, "that person is not beyond the jurisdiction of the courts".

16
00:02:15,567 --> 00:02:20,917
It added that such theories "should be rejected
summarily, however they are presented".

17
00:02:20,933 --> 00:02:39,221
Courts in New Zealand heard the same argument from people who said a court's authority depended on their consent, and all three lost; the researchers who
reviewed those cases put the problem simply: "no one needs to explicitly and affirmatively consent to the authority of a jurisdiction to be subject to it".

18
00:02:39,733 --> 00:02:41,083
Then there is the flag.

19
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:49,088
A letter in the Mountain Democrat newspaper states the belief
in one line: "The fringe denotes Admiralty law's jurisdiction".

20
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:59,237
A federal court, noting that other courts had already called the argument frivolous,
decided to examine the law of the flag "in the interest of killing this argument for good".

21
00:02:59,567 --> 00:03:08,879
It found that the Army's own Adjutant General had written that a fringe "is not
considered to be a part of the flag, and it is without heraldic significance".

22
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:22,075
Then it said what decides the question: "Jurisdiction is a matter of law, statute, and constitution, not
a child's game wherein one's power is magnified or diminished by the display of some magic talisman".

23
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:28,350
The next trick goes bigger: it says the whole
country was quietly turned into a company.

24
00:03:28,667 --> 00:03:37,242
The channel SYDLIS says "the act of 1871 that is exactly what
changed america from a constitutional republic to a corporation".

25
00:03:37,267 --> 00:03:52,592
The act of 1871 is real, and the Supreme Court later described what it did: it made the District of Columbia "a body
corporate for municipal purposes", a local government that could make contracts, sue and be sued, and look after its streets.

26
00:03:52,900 --> 00:04:02,075
The Court called the District "a mere municipal corporation", and said the
sovereign power over it was lodged "in the government of the United States".

27
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:09,850
And the government that the 1871 act set up did
not even last: an act of 1874 abolished it.

28
00:04:10,867 --> 00:04:13,592
The last item in the kit is the broadest.

29
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:23,750
Americans In Action says that "these codes and statutes are only the bylaws
of the corporation", rules that, it says, do not bind state nationals.

30
00:04:23,767 --> 00:04:35,729
In Bey v. Indiana, a man argued that Indiana could not tax him without a contract between
them, and the federal appeals court answered: "We have repeatedly rejected such claims".

31
00:04:35,733 --> 00:04:46,521
In New Zealand, a self-described freeman said he had never consented to the Land Transport
Act and so was not bound by it; the court struck out his claim as an abuse of process.

32
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:56,121
The reason is simple: "Everyone who operates a vehicle on public roads
must comply with legislation regulating the operation of vehicles".

33
00:04:56,133 --> 00:05:04,996
So the answer to the first question is no: courts in the United States, Canada
and New Zealand have rejected the idea that their power depends on your consent.

34
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:18,863
Researchers who followed these arguments to the Supreme Court of Canada found that it has never granted
one of them leave to appeal, and their verdict is blunt: "These ideas are pseudolaw, and devoid of merit".

35
00:05:18,867 --> 00:05:31,892
A review of cases in Australia and New Zealand reached the same place: "No courts accept these arguments,
and no courts absolve the claimant (or defendant) from their legal obligations and responsibilities".

36
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:43,550
For the United States, the evidence here has no count like that; what it has is a run of rulings, and the
Ohio appeals court summed them up by saying the claims "continue to make their way to court and fail".

37
00:05:43,867 --> 00:05:51,079
If nothing you say, sign or point to removes a court's
power, the promoters' next offer is to change who you are.

38
00:05:52,100 --> 00:06:02,175
A channel called Sovereign National describes ordinary citizenship as the thing "which
forces you into their statutes and codes and bylaws and we are not part of that".

39
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:13,225
Some sellers promise that the paperwork works at the roadside too: SPC University tells viewers
that "when the officer runs your id he or she will see your status and has to let you go".

40
00:06:13,233 --> 00:06:15,858
So what is a national, in the law itself?

41
00:06:16,167 --> 00:06:27,254
Federal statute defines a national of the United States as "a citizen of the United
States", or a person who is not a citizen but owes the country permanent allegiance.

42
00:06:27,267 --> 00:06:39,567
So every citizen is already a national, and the non-citizen kind comes from the circumstances of
birth, such as being born in an outlying possession of the United States, not from a form you file.

43
00:06:39,567 --> 00:06:53,104
Federal appeals courts, in tax cases, have also rejected the related claim that being a citizen of a state rather
than of the United States puts a person beyond the federal courts; one called it "barely worth a footnote".

44
00:06:53,133 --> 00:07:07,346
The Moorish version leans on old treaties instead, and the Institute for Strategic Dialogue explains how: "Moorish sovereigns
cite that treaty to claim that Moors in the US have sovereign status, meaning they would be exempt from normal laws".

45
00:07:07,367 --> 00:07:17,079
In Bey v. Indiana, the man who argued that being a Moor made him a
sovereign citizen wanted an $11.5 billion refund from the State of Indiana.

46
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:27,975
The court said the treaty argument is made "without any basis in fact", and that Bey "is
a U.S. citizen and therefore unlike foreign diplomats has no immunity from U.S. law".

47
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:42,988
The oldest-sounding version reaches back to the common law, and the National Liberty Alliance teaches that "the un-codified
common law is the superior law of the people and the codified civil law is the special or inferior law of the government".

48
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:49,163
As for Magna Carta, it had been repealed
by 1969 except for three provisions.

49
00:07:49,167 --> 00:08:00,117
As early as 1905, the High Court of Australia said the claim that a law is invalid
because it does not conform to Magna Carta "is not one for serious refutation".

50
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:10,383
And the Supreme Court of Canada has refused to hear appeals built on Magna Carta and
historical English common law, which the researchers read as a quiet rejection of both.

51
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:31,987
On the papers that are meant to stop police, the evidence is thin: one FBI bulletin reports that three people in Kansas City were convicted for selling "sovereign
status" ID cards, at $450 to $2,000 each, that promised immunity from stops and arrests, and it records the sellers' convictions, not what happened to buyers at a stop.

52
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:43,563
So the answer to the second question is no as well: no court in this evidence has accepted
a declared status, whether state national, Moor or free man under the common law.

53
00:08:43,867 --> 00:08:55,792
The parts settled less directly are narrow: the federal rulings on state citizenship come from tax cases,
so a state court's reach is not what they tested, and the stop question rests on the bulletin above.

54
00:08:56,800 --> 00:09:06,938
Both answers come down to the same thing: a court gets its power from the law, not from
what you write under your name, what hangs on its wall, or what you declare yourself to be.

55
00:09:06,967 --> 00:09:14,854
So if someone offers you a form, a code to write by your signature,
or a card for the glovebox, ask them for one ruling where it worked.

56
00:09:14,867 --> 00:09:20,529
The rulings where it was tried are public, and they
are the place to look before you sign anything.
